# Line endings: LF everywhere, in the index AND in the working tree.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# Every text blob in this repo is already stored LF (verified: `git ls-files
# --eol` reported 1553 i/lf and 0 i/crlf). What was NOT pinned is CHECKOUT:
# with the common Windows setting `core.autocrlf=true`, git rewrites those LF
# files to CRLF on disk, and then `pnpm eslint .` reports `Delete ␍` on
# essentially every line of ~1450 files — prettier defaults to `endOfLine: lf`.
#
# The practical cost was not cosmetic: a whole-repo lint run drowned in
# hundreds of newline errors, so it could not be used to find real problems,
# and a file edited by a tool that writes CRLF looked identical to one that was
# fine. `.gitattributes` beats `core.autocrlf`, so pinning it here fixes the
# checkout for every clone without asking anyone to reconfigure their git.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` — let git detect text vs binary (so it never mangles a
# .png or a .exe), and for anything it calls text, use LF on both sides.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts that Windows tooling may require CRLF for. Batch files
# genuinely misbehave with LF line endings in some shells (a trailing LF in a
# `goto` label or a multi-line `if` block can be misparsed), and PowerShell is
# fine either way but ships CRLF by convention on Windows.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# NSIS installer scripts are consumed by makensis on Windows.
*.nsh  text eol=crlf
*.nsi  text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch. Listed explicitly rather than relying on detection —
# a mis-detected binary is silently corrupted, and these are the extensions
# this repo actually carries (icons, fonts, sidecar binaries, snapshots).
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.so   binary
*.dylib binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.msi  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.heapsnapshot binary
